window.__DATA=window.__DATA||{};window.__DATA['bank/2023_12_3']={"id":"2023_12_3","title":"2023年12月 第3套","year":2023,"month":12,"set":"3","readingOnly":false,"hasAudio":true,"audio":"audio/b1a4abe1.mp3","audioDuration":1417.3,"audioMissing":false,"counts":{"total":55,"listening":25,"cloze":10,"matching":10,"reading":10},"passages":{"passage1":{"kind":"passage","text":"In the history of horse racing, few horses have captured the affection of the British public like Red Rum. Today, three decades after his retirement, he is still one of the best-known and most beloved racehorses of all time. Red Rum was passed around to several owners before being purchased for Noel Le Mar. The agent who made the purchase was the now-legendary horse trainer Donald \"Ginger\" McCain. It wasn't apparent at the time, but Red Rum had a serious bone disease in his foot. For many horses (and many trainers) this would mean the end of a racing career before it even began. For Ginger and Red Rum, though, it was just an obstacle to greatness that had to be overcome. Red Rum's true talent came out in steeplechases (障碍赛马). His power, speed and jumping ability carried him to his first Grand National title in 1973. The very next year, Red Rum returned to take his second title. He was the first horse to take successive firsts since Reynoldstown in 1935-1936. Red Rum's spirit and grace had already begun to charm the leagues of Grand National fans. In the following two years, Red Rum lost out on the title, coming in second both times. When he returned in 1977 to try again, he was largely regarded as past his prime. He was 12 years old and not expected to place highly. He surprised sporting fans around the world when he came in a remarkable 25 lengths ahead of the nearest horse, taking his third Grand National win. To this day, Red Rum's third win is known as one of the greatest moments in horse racing history. Red Rum was headed for the Grand National once again in 1978 but suffered an injury in one of his heels shortly before the race. He was retired soon after, but his public life and fame by no means decreased with the end of his career. Red Rum traveled all over the country for various engagements. He often led pre-race parades at Aintree Racecourse and was a popular guest at charity benefits and public events."},"passage2":{"kind":"passage","text":"People in business often make decisions based on their own personal feelings or instincts. It is quite horrifying to see people being guided by some unknown force. But how wise is it, really, to let your instincts drive your decisions? In the decision-making process, relying on instincts only makes sense when you have a vast experience to support you. Simply \"feeling\" that something is right or should be done is highly subjective and can drown you. On the other hand, there is a more rational approach to making decisions. Data and analysis have long been associated with informed decisions. These reduce the likelihood of errors and increase the chances of success. Big, systematic data is mostly the foundation of most of our decisions, personal and business alike. But with its extreme usability comes a complication: what do you do if you strongly feel something should not be done, even if the data insists that it should? This seemingly easy question is what drives the need to understand the relationship between instincts and data in the decision-making process. Without making things complicated, the solution to this dilemma is using data and instincts in conjunction to arrive at the best possible decisions. Instinctive decisions are always backed by previous experiences or information, which acknowledges that instinctive decisions have worked in the past. Decisions are not about making the choice and braving the consequences because you want to blindly trust your instincts. They are about combining your inner wisdom with the knowledge of systematic data to make the best decisions. Sometimes, rational analysis and data are impractical to be employed in certain situations. Absence of definitive criteria, and time and resource constraints, and novel situations are instances which limit the practicality of data. The only feasible option is to rely on what your instincts tell you. In these situations, instincts can help you make effective and quick decisions. Combining instinctive and rational analysis produces well-rounded decisions. It reduces the chances of making mistakes, and has increasingly become a favorite approach to decision-making among leaders of today."},"cloze":{"kind":"cloze","text":"with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. When people set out to improve their health, they usually take a familiar path: starting a healthy diet, getting better sleep, and doing regular exercise. Each of these behaviors is important, of course, but they all ___26___ on physical health—and a growing body of research suggests that social health is just as, if not more, important to ___27___ well-being. One recent study published in the journal PLOS ONE, for example, found that the strength of a person's social circle was a better ___28___ of self-reported stress, happiness and well-being levels than fitness tracker data on physical activity, heart rate and sleep. That finding suggests that the \" ___29___ self \" represented by endless amounts of health data doesn't tell the whole ___30___. There's also a qualified self, which is who I am, what are my activities, my social network, and all of these aspects are not ___31___ in any of these measurements. This idea is supported by plenty of ___32___ research. Studies have shown that social support—whether it comes from friends, family members or a spouse—is ___33___ associated with better mental and physical health. A rich social life, these studies suggest, can lower stress levels, improve mood, encourage positive health behaviors and discourage damaging ones, boost heart health and improve illness ___34___ rates. Social isolation, meanwhile, is linked to higher rates of physical diseases and mental health conditions. It's a significant problem, ___35___ since loneliness is emerging as a widespread public health problem in many countries.","wordBank":{"A":"base","B":"eagerly","C":"especially","D":"focus","E":"indicator","F":"overall","G":"preached","H":"prior","I":"prompt","J":"puzzle","K":"quantified","L":"recovery","M":"reflected","N":"story","O":"strongly"}},"matching":{"kind":"matching","text":"","paragraphs":{"A":"As a parent of two boys at primary school, I worry about the issues associated with teenagers and social media. Newspapers are constantly filled with frightening accounts of drug addiction and aggressive behaviour supposedly caused by violent videogames. But even when these accounts touch on real concerns, they do not really reflect the great mass of everyday teenage social behaviour: the online chat, the texting, the surfing, and the emergence of a new teenage sphere that is conducted digitally.","B":"New technologies always provoke generational panic, which usually has more to do with adult fears than with the lives of teenagers. In the 1930s, parents worried that radio was gaining \"an irresistible hold of their children\". In the 80s, the great danger was the Sony Walkman (随身听). When you look at today's digital activity, the facts are much more positive than you might expect.","C":"Indeed, social scientists who study young people have found that their digital use can be inventive and even beneficial. This is true not just in terms of their social lives, but their education too. So if you use a ton of social media, do you become unable, or unwilling, to engage in face-to-face contact? The evidence suggests not. Research by Amanda Lenhart of the Pew Research Centre, a US think tank, found that the most passionate texters are also the kids most likely to spend time with friends in person. One form of socialising doesn't replace the other. It expands it.","D":"\"Kids still spend time face to face,\" Lenhart says. Indeed, as they get older and are given more freedom, they often ease up on social networking. Early on, the web is their “third space”, but by the late teens, it's replaced in reaction to greater independence. They have to be on Facebook, to know what's going on among friends and family, but they are ambivalent (有矛盾心理的) about it, says Rebecca Eynon, a research fellow at the Oxford Internet Institute, who has interviewed about 200 British teenagers over three years. As they gain experience with living online, they begin to adjust their behaviour, struggling with new communication skills, as they do in the real world.","E":"Parents are wrong to worry that kids don't care about privacy. In fact, they spend hours changing Facebook settings or using quick-delete sharing tools, such as Snapchat, to minimise their traces. Or they post a photograph on Instagram, have a pleasant conversation with friends and then delete it so that no traces remain.","F":"This is not to say that kids always use good judgment. Like everyone else, they make mistakes—sometimes serious ones. But working out how to behave online is a new social skill. While there's plenty of drama and messiness online, it is not, for most teens, a cycle of non-stop abuse: a Pew study found only 15% of teens said someone had bullied them online in the last 12 months.","G":"But surely all this short-form writing is affecting literacy? Certainly, teachers worry. They say that kids use overly casual language and text-speak in writing, and don't have as much patience for long reading and complex arguments. Yet studies of first-year college papers suggest these anxieties may be partly based on misguided nostalgia (怀旧). When Stanford University scholar Andrea Lunsford gathered data on the rates of errors in “freshman composition” papers going back to 1917, she found that they were virtually identical to today.","H":"But even as error rates stayed stable, student essays have blossomed in size and complexity. They are now six times longer and, unlike older \"what I did this summer” essays, they offer arguments supported by evidence. Why? Computers have vastly increased the ability of students to gather information, sample different points of view and write more fluidly.","I":"When linguist Naomi Baron studied students' instant messaging even there she found surprisingly rare usage of short forms such as \"u” for \"you\", and as students got older, they began to write in more grammatical sentences. That is because they want to appear more adult, and they know how adults are expected to write. Clearly, teaching teens formal writing is still crucial, but texting probably isn't destroying their ability to learn it.","J":"It is probably true that fewer kids are heavy readers compared with two generations ago, when cheap paperbacks boosted rates of reading. But even back then, a minority of people—perhaps 20%—were lifelong heavy readers, and it was cable TV, not the internet, that struck a blow at that culture in the 1980s. Still, 15%or more of kids are found to be deeply bookish. In fact, the online world offers kids remarkable opportunities to become literate and creative because young people can now publish ideas not just to their friends, but to the world. And it turns out that when they write for strangers, their sense of \"authentic audience\" makes them work harder, push themselves further, and create powerful new communicative forms.","K":"Few would deny that too much time online can be harmful. Some of the dangers are emotional: hurting someone from a distance is not the same as hurting them face to face. If we're lucky, the legal environment will change to make teenagers' online lives less likely to haunt them later on. Just last week, California passed a law allowing minors to demand that internet firms erase their digital past and the EU has considered similar legislation.","L":"Distraction is also a serious issue. When kids switch from chat to music to homework, they are indeed likely to have trouble doing each task well. And studies show that pupils don't fact-check information online-\"smart searching” is a skill schools need to teach urgently. It's also true that too much social networking and game playing can cut into schoolwork and sleep. This is precisely why parents still need to set firm boundaries around it, as with any other distraction. M) So what's the best way to cope? The same boring old advice that applies to everything in parenting: moderation. Rebecca Eynon argues that it's key to model good behaviour. Parents who stare non-stop at their phones and don't read books are likely to breed kids who will do the same. As ever, we ought to be careful about our own behaviour."},"letters":["A","B","C","D","E","F","G","H","I","J","K","L"]}},"questions":[{"no":1,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"1","options":{"A":"He noticed the driver was too young to drive.","B":"He found there was no one sitting at the wheel.","C":"He thought something must be wrong with the driver.","D":"He saw the driver changing lanes much too frequently."},"script":"A police officer in the U.S. stopped a large SUV car that was going very slowly and drifting across lanes on a\nhighway. [1] He expected to find a driver who was either very drunk or having a medical emergency. Instead, the\nofficer discovered a five-year-old boy sat on the edge of the driver's seat. His feet could barely reach the brake, and\nhis head was only just high enough to see out of the windows. The child had taken the keys to the family car while his\nteenage sister was napping. He then drove three kilometers across town before getting on the highway. [2] The boy later told confused officers that he was planning to travel to California and buy a Lamborghini sports car. Although he only had three dollars in his wallet, at least he was driving in the right direction.","audioStart":43.1,"groupNote":"Questions 1 to 2","answer":"C","expl":"新闻开头提到，警官拦下了一辆行驶缓慢且横穿公路车道的大型SUV，他本以为会发现司机要么喝得酪酊大醉，要么存在医疗紧急情况，即“认为司机一定出了问题”，故C项正确。A项“注意到司机太年轻”、B项“发现方向盘上没有人”、D项“看到司机频繁变道”均未在新闻中提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻细节捕捉，重点关注“expect to find”引导的预判性内容。"},{"no":2,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"2","options":{"A":"Buy a sports car.","B":"Drive across town.","C":"Leave California.","D":"Visit his sister."},"script":"A police officer in the U.S. stopped a large SUV car that was going very slowly and drifting across lanes on a\nhighway. [1] He expected to find a driver who was either very drunk or having a medical emergency. Instead, the\nofficer discovered a five-year-old boy sat on the edge of the driver's seat. His feet could barely reach the brake, and\nhis head was only just high enough to see out of the windows. The child had taken the keys to the family car while his\nteenage sister was napping. He then drove three kilometers across town before getting on the highway. [2] The boy later told confused officers that he was planning to travel to California and buy a Lamborghini sports car. Although he only had three dollars in his wallet, at least he was driving in the right direction.","audioStart":43.1,"groupNote":"Questions 1 to 2","answer":"A","expl":"新闻末尾明确提到，男孩告诉困惑的警官，他打算去加利福尼亚买一辆兰博基尼跑车，A项“买一辆跑车”与原文一致。B项“开车穿过小镇”是男孩的行为过程而非目的；C项“离开加利福尼亚”与原文“前往加利福尼亚”相反；D项“去看望他的姐姐”无依据（男孩是趁姐姐打瞌睡拿走车钥匙），排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻中“plan to do”表目的的细节定位，区分行为过程与核心目的。"},{"no":3,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"3","options":{"A":"How they change the way we shop.","B":"How they alter human skeletons.","C":"How they cause increased headaches.","D":"How they affect our communication."},"script":"Mobile phones have changed the way we live, how we read, work, communicate, and shop. But we already\nknow this. [3] What we have not yet understood is the way the tiny machines in front of us are changing our skeletons,\npossibly altering not just the way we behave but even the very shape of our bodies.\nNew scientific research at the University of the Sunshine Coast in Queensland, Australia, suggests that young people\nare developing extra pieces of bone at the backs of their heads. These pieces of bone are caused by the way people bend their\nheads when they use the phone. This shifts weight onto the muscles at the back of the head and causes the bone to grow in\na way that is not normal. [4] This process can be compared to the way the skin hardens in response to constant rubbing or\npressure. The result is a piece of bone like a horn that sticks out from the head by the neck.","audioStart":145.6,"groupNote":"Questions 3 to 4","answer":"B","expl":"新闻开头指出，手机改变了人们的阅读、工作、交流和购物方式（这些是已知的），但尚未了解的是“这些小机器如何改变我们的骨骼”，故B项正确。A项“改变购物方式”、D项“影响交流”均为已知信息；C项“导致头痛加剧”未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻中“what we have not yet understood”表未知信息的定位，区分已知与未知内容。"},{"no":4,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"4","options":{"A":"It loosens.","B":"It brightens.","C":"It hardens.","D":"It softens."},"script":"Mobile phones have changed the way we live, how we read, work, communicate, and shop. But we already\nknow this. [3] What we have not yet understood is the way the tiny machines in front of us are changing our skeletons,\npossibly altering not just the way we behave but even the very shape of our bodies.\nNew scientific research at the University of the Sunshine Coast in Queensland, Australia, suggests that young people\nare developing extra pieces of bone at the backs of their heads. These pieces of bone are caused by the way people bend their\nheads when they use the phone. This shifts weight onto the muscles at the back of the head and causes the bone to grow in\na way that is not normal. [4] This process can be compared to the way the skin hardens in response to constant rubbing or\npressure. The result is a piece of bone like a horn that sticks out from the head by the neck.","audioStart":145.6,"groupNote":"Questions 3 to 4","answer":"C","expl":"新闻提到，长期用手机低头导致后脑勺长额外骨头的过程，可比作“皮肤在持续摩擦或压力下变硬的过程”，直接说明皮肤在该情况下会变硬，C项正确。A项“变松”、B项“变明亮”、D项“变软”均未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻中类比关系（be compared to）的理解与细节提取。"},{"no":5,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"5","options":{"A":"Create Internet pages for him.","B":"Ask a local pet shop to adopt him.","C":"Name an orange tree after him.","D":"Hold a birthday party for him."},"script":"[5] A village is going to throw a birthday party for an orange cat named Wilbur, who has become famous for\nmaking a regular appearance at local shops.\nWilbur has his own Internet pages as he approaches his 10th birthday on July, 7. Wilbur is both bold and friendly.\n[6] He spends most of his time in shops and businesses around the village. He'll just take himself into any shop,\nanywhere. There was one time when the doctor's receptionist came back and he was sitting on her chair. Among his\nfavorite haunts are a local hairdresser, two pubs, and the Nottingham Primary School.\nLesley Harper, who runs one of the pubs, said the cat has been a big hit with their customers.\"He's been a regular visitor\nfor most of this year,she told the Press Association.\"He's a very relaxed cat, happy for customers and villagers young and\nold to come in and say hello.\"[7] She also said he is a cat of expensive tastes who likes his high priced cat food.\nSarah Godfrey, who is organizing Wilbur's party, told the local newspaper, \"Wilbur is part of our lives, as he is for\neveryone who lives in the village.\"","audioStart":262.4,"groupNote":"Questions 5 to 7","answer":"D","expl":"新闻开头明确说明“一个村庄要为名叫威尔伯的橘猫举办生日派对”，D项正确。A项“创建网页”是猫已有的情况，并非村庄要做的事；B项“请宠物店收养”、C项“用它的名字命名橘子树”均未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻主旨细节捕捉，关注“be going to do”表计划的内容。"},{"no":6,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"6","options":{"A":"He is a bold and aggressive pet.","B":"He pays regular visits to village shops.","C":"He once bit a doctor's receptionist.","D":"He likes to sit on the hairdresser's chair."},"script":"[5] A village is going to throw a birthday party for an orange cat named Wilbur, who has become famous for\nmaking a regular appearance at local shops.\nWilbur has his own Internet pages as he approaches his 10th birthday on July, 7. Wilbur is both bold and friendly.\n[6] He spends most of his time in shops and businesses around the village. He'll just take himself into any shop,\nanywhere. There was one time when the doctor's receptionist came back and he was sitting on her chair. Among his\nfavorite haunts are a local hairdresser, two pubs, and the Nottingham Primary School.\nLesley Harper, who runs one of the pubs, said the cat has been a big hit with their customers.\"He's been a regular visitor\nfor most of this year,she told the Press Association.\"He's a very relaxed cat, happy for customers and villagers young and\nold to come in and say hello.\"[7] She also said he is a cat of expensive tastes who likes his high priced cat food.\nSarah Godfrey, who is organizing Wilbur's party, told the local newspaper, \"Wilbur is part of our lives, as he is for\neveryone who lives in the village.\"","audioStart":262.4,"groupNote":"Questions 5 to 7","answer":"B","expl":"新闻提到，这只猫“大部分时间都待在村周边的商店和企业里，会自己走进任何一家商店”，即“经常光顾村里的商店”，B项正确。A项“大胆而好斗”与原文“大胆而友好”不符；C项“咬过医生接待员”无依据（仅提到猫坐在接待员椅子上）；D项“喜欢坐在理发师椅子上”未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻中描述事物特征的细节辨析，排除与原文相悖或无依据的选项。"},{"no":7,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"7","options":{"A":"He is fond of luxury cat food.","B":"He likes to stay in villagers' houses.","C":"He knows everybody in the village.","D":"He often seeks food around her pub."},"script":"[5] A village is going to throw a birthday party for an orange cat named Wilbur, who has become famous for\nmaking a regular appearance at local shops.\nWilbur has his own Internet pages as he approaches his 10th birthday on July, 7. Wilbur is both bold and friendly.\n[6] He spends most of his time in shops and businesses around the village. He'll just take himself into any shop,\nanywhere. There was one time when the doctor's receptionist came back and he was sitting on her chair. Among his\nfavorite haunts are a local hairdresser, two pubs, and the Nottingham Primary School.\nLesley Harper, who runs one of the pubs, said the cat has been a big hit with their customers.\"He's been a regular visitor\nfor most of this year,she told the Press Association.\"He's a very relaxed cat, happy for customers and villagers young and\nold to come in and say hello.\"[7] She also said he is a cat of expensive tastes who likes his high priced cat food.\nSarah Godfrey, who is organizing Wilbur's party, told the local newspaper, \"Wilbur is part of our lives, as he is for\neveryone who lives in the village.\"","audioStart":262.4,"groupNote":"Questions 5 to 7","answer":"A","expl":"酒吧老板莱斯利·哈珀提到“这只猫口味奢侈，喜欢高价猫粮”，A项正确。B项“喜欢住在村民家里”、C项“认识村里每个人”、D项“经常在酒吧附近觅食”均未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力新闻中人物观点的细节提取，关注“sb. said”引导的内容。"},{"no":8,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"8","options":{"A":"Who to order the food.","B":"Whether to have sandwiches.","C":"When to go for their meal.","D":"Where to have their lunch."},"script":"M: So, [8] where do you want to go for lunch?\nW: I don't know. Do you have anything in mind?\nM: [9] What about the Sandwich Place on Camden Street?\nW: Hmm... No, sorry. I don't feel like sandwiches today. It's a great place, but I think we go there too often.\nM: That's true. [9] Remember that's where we saw Brigette Clark, the famous movie star.\nW: Of course. How could I ever forget? There were crowds of people around her asking for a photo with her.\nM: What about hamburgers, then? There's that American style diner on the way to the mall.\nW: I like that place. Their chips are great, but their service takes a long time. [10] And I need to get back by 2:30 for a\nconference call. How about trying something new? We could try Mario's, the new Italian restaurant on the corner. It\nlooks all right. Have you been there?\nM: No, I haven't, but I've noticed that it has great reviews on the Internet. It's supposed to be one of the best Italian's\nin town. I think Jeremy has been there and said it was amazing. I'm up for that.\nW:Cool.Have you asked Jeremy if he would like to come with us?\nM: I already have.He says he can't. He has brought his own lunch today, so he will stay in. I think his wife made him\nsoup.\nW: Poor Jeremy. [ll] His wife is a terrible cook. He should throw that soup in the bin and join us.","audioStart":443.3,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"D","expl":"对话开头男士直接询问“你想去哪儿吃午饭”，后续围绕“三明治店、美式餐厅、意大利餐厅”等选址展开，核心话题是“去哪儿吃午饭”，D项正确。B项“要不要吃三明治”是对话中的一个细节而非核心主题；A项“谁去点菜”、C项“什么时候去吃饭”未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话主旨概括，关注开头提问及后续讨论的核心对象。"},{"no":9,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"9","options":{"A":"In the shopping center nearby.","B":"In the expensive Italian style diner.","C":"At the Sandwich Place on Camden Street.","D":"At the American restaurant they frequent."},"script":"M: So, [8] where do you want to go for lunch?\nW: I don't know. Do you have anything in mind?\nM: [9] What about the Sandwich Place on Camden Street?\nW: Hmm... No, sorry. I don't feel like sandwiches today. It's a great place, but I think we go there too often.\nM: That's true. [9] Remember that's where we saw Brigette Clark, the famous movie star.\nW: Of course. How could I ever forget? There were crowds of people around her asking for a photo with her.\nM: What about hamburgers, then? There's that American style diner on the way to the mall.\nW: I like that place. Their chips are great, but their service takes a long time. [10] And I need to get back by 2:30 for a\nconference call. How about trying something new? We could try Mario's, the new Italian restaurant on the corner. It\nlooks all right. Have you been there?\nM: No, I haven't, but I've noticed that it has great reviews on the Internet. It's supposed to be one of the best Italian's\nin town. I think Jeremy has been there and said it was amazing. I'm up for that.\nW:Cool.Have you asked Jeremy if he would like to come with us?\nM: I already have.He says he can't. He has brought his own lunch today, so he will stay in. I think his wife made him\nsoup.\nW: Poor Jeremy. [ll] His wife is a terrible cook. He should throw that soup in the bin and join us.","audioStart":443.3,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"C","expl":"男士提议去“卡姆登街的三明治店”，女士拒绝后，男士补充“记得我们在那里见过著名影星布里奇特·克拉克”，明确说明见面地点是该三明治店，C项正确。A项“附近购物中心”、B项“昂贵的意式餐厅”、D项“常去的美式餐厅”均与原文不符，排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中地点信息的细节定位，关联提议与回忆的逻辑关系。"},{"no":10,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"10","options":{"A":"There is to be a conference call.","B":"She has to meet with her boss then.","C":"There will be crowds of people waiting for her.","D":"She will have a photo taken with Brigette Clark."},"script":"M: So, [8] where do you want to go for lunch?\nW: I don't know. Do you have anything in mind?\nM: [9] What about the Sandwich Place on Camden Street?\nW: Hmm... No, sorry. I don't feel like sandwiches today. It's a great place, but I think we go there too often.\nM: That's true. [9] Remember that's where we saw Brigette Clark, the famous movie star.\nW: Of course. How could I ever forget? There were crowds of people around her asking for a photo with her.\nM: What about hamburgers, then? There's that American style diner on the way to the mall.\nW: I like that place. Their chips are great, but their service takes a long time. [10] And I need to get back by 2:30 for a\nconference call. How about trying something new? We could try Mario's, the new Italian restaurant on the corner. It\nlooks all right. Have you been there?\nM: No, I haven't, but I've noticed that it has great reviews on the Internet. It's supposed to be one of the best Italian's\nin town. I think Jeremy has been there and said it was amazing. I'm up for that.\nW:Cool.Have you asked Jeremy if he would like to come with us?\nM: I already have.He says he can't. He has brought his own lunch today, so he will stay in. I think his wife made him\nsoup.\nW: Poor Jeremy. [ll] His wife is a terrible cook. He should throw that soup in the bin and join us.","audioStart":443.3,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"A","expl":"女士评价美式餐厅时提到“上菜时间太久，我得在2:30之前回来参加电话会议”，直接说明原因是“有个电话会议”，A项正确。B项“见老板”、C项“很多人等她”、D项“与影星合影”均未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中“need to do”的原因细节捕捉，关注“and”引导的补充说明内容。"},{"no":11,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"11","options":{"A":"She doesn't deem homemade soup tasty.","B":"She doesn't think his wife cooks well.","C":"She feels Jeremy would rather dine out.","D":"She has found the soup smells terrible."},"script":"M: So, [8] where do you want to go for lunch?\nW: I don't know. Do you have anything in mind?\nM: [9] What about the Sandwich Place on Camden Street?\nW: Hmm... No, sorry. I don't feel like sandwiches today. It's a great place, but I think we go there too often.\nM: That's true. [9] Remember that's where we saw Brigette Clark, the famous movie star.\nW: Of course. How could I ever forget? There were crowds of people around her asking for a photo with her.\nM: What about hamburgers, then? There's that American style diner on the way to the mall.\nW: I like that place. Their chips are great, but their service takes a long time. [10] And I need to get back by 2:30 for a\nconference call. How about trying something new? We could try Mario's, the new Italian restaurant on the corner. It\nlooks all right. Have you been there?\nM: No, I haven't, but I've noticed that it has great reviews on the Internet. It's supposed to be one of the best Italian's\nin town. I think Jeremy has been there and said it was amazing. I'm up for that.\nW:Cool.Have you asked Jeremy if he would like to come with us?\nM: I already have.He says he can't. He has brought his own lunch today, so he will stay in. I think his wife made him\nsoup.\nW: Poor Jeremy. [ll] His wife is a terrible cook. He should throw that soup in the bin and join us.","audioStart":443.3,"groupNote":"Questions 8 to 11","answer":"B","expl":"男士提到“杰里米的妻子给她做了汤”，女士回应“他妻子厨艺太糟糕了，他应该把汤扔进垃圾桶”，核心原因是“觉得他妻子做饭不好吃”，B项正确。A项“家里煮的汤不好喝”范围过广（原文特指杰里米妻子做的汤）；C项“杰里米更愿意外出吃饭”、D项“汤味道难闻”未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中因果关系的细节提取，关注人物评价与建议的逻辑关联。"},{"no":12,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"12","options":{"A":"A landlady.","B":"A waitress.","C":"A receptionist.","D":"A saleswoman."},"script":"W: Hi, there. How are you today? [12] Do you have a reservation with us already?\nM: Good afternoon. [12] Yes, we reserved our rooms yesterday morning on your website for three nights. The name's\nPatterson.\nW: Okay. Let me have a look. Yes, we have it here. You brought the whole family with you, I see\nM: Yes, the two kids, my wife and I, and her parents too.\nW: Great. So we have a family room for you and your wife and the kids, and another double room for your parents-\nin-law. They are right next to each other on the ground floor, since you mentioned in your message that they have\ntrouble with stairs.\nM: That's wonderful. [13] My father-in-law has had terrible problems getting up and down stairs since his knee\noperation last April.\nW: I'm sorry to hear that. And if you need any help to find transportation for the whole family, we can definitely\nrecommend someone for you.\nM:We were thinking of renting a car, but we will explore all the options available for sure.[14] So, yes, that would be\nvery helpful in comparing prices. We're also wondering what tours and day trips are available.\nW: We have bunches of brochures here. [15] I would recommend getting out on a boat trip. The kids will love it and\nthere are so many islands nearby to explore. There's also a great night market further into town that has all kinds of\nfood and cool little shops selling souvenirs and local jewelry and clothing made by hand.\nM:That all sounds marvelous.\nW: [12] Now, all I need is to photocopy your passports and then I can get you all checked in and show you to your\nrooms.","audioStart":615.8,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"C","expl":"对话开头女士询问“是否已有预订”，男士回应“前一天在网站预订了三晚房间”，结尾女士提出“复印护照、办理入住并带往房间”，这些均是酒店前台接待员的典型工作内容，故C项正确。A项“房东太太”、B项“女服务员”、D项“女售货员”的工作场景与对话内容不符，排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中职业身份推断，根据工作内容（预订确认、入住办理、护照复印）锁定职业。"},{"no":13,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"13","options":{"A":"He was involved in a terrible car accident last April.","B":"He has much difficulty getting up and down stairs.","C":"He is expected to undergo a knee operation.","D":"He prefers to stay next door to the children."},"script":"W: Hi, there. How are you today? [12] Do you have a reservation with us already?\nM: Good afternoon. [12] Yes, we reserved our rooms yesterday morning on your website for three nights. The name's\nPatterson.\nW: Okay. Let me have a look. Yes, we have it here. You brought the whole family with you, I see\nM: Yes, the two kids, my wife and I, and her parents too.\nW: Great. So we have a family room for you and your wife and the kids, and another double room for your parents-\nin-law. They are right next to each other on the ground floor, since you mentioned in your message that they have\ntrouble with stairs.\nM: That's wonderful. [13] My father-in-law has had terrible problems getting up and down stairs since his knee\noperation last April.\nW: I'm sorry to hear that. And if you need any help to find transportation for the whole family, we can definitely\nrecommend someone for you.\nM:We were thinking of renting a car, but we will explore all the options available for sure.[14] So, yes, that would be\nvery helpful in comparing prices. We're also wondering what tours and day trips are available.\nW: We have bunches of brochures here. [15] I would recommend getting out on a boat trip. The kids will love it and\nthere are so many islands nearby to explore. There's also a great night market further into town that has all kinds of\nfood and cool little shops selling souvenirs and local jewelry and clothing made by hand.\nM:That all sounds marvelous.\nW: [12] Now, all I need is to photocopy your passports and then I can get you all checked in and show you to your\nrooms.","audioStart":615.8,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"B","expl":"男士提到“岳父去年四月做了膝关节手术后，上下楼梯就成了大问题”，即“上下楼梯非常困难”，B项正确。A项“遭遇严重车祸”未提及；C项“预计做膝关节手术”与原文“已做过手术”不符；D项“喜欢住在孩子隔壁”无依据（房间相邻是因为爬楼梯不便），排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中人物情况的细节提取，区分“已发生”与“未发生”的时间逻辑。"},{"no":14,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"14","options":{"A":"To please his parents-in-law.","B":"To find the best trip for his kids.","C":"To satisfy his curiosity.","D":"To compare prices."},"script":"W: Hi, there. How are you today? [12] Do you have a reservation with us already?\nM: Good afternoon. [12] Yes, we reserved our rooms yesterday morning on your website for three nights. The name's\nPatterson.\nW: Okay. Let me have a look. Yes, we have it here. You brought the whole family with you, I see\nM: Yes, the two kids, my wife and I, and her parents too.\nW: Great. So we have a family room for you and your wife and the kids, and another double room for your parents-\nin-law. They are right next to each other on the ground floor, since you mentioned in your message that they have\ntrouble with stairs.\nM: That's wonderful. [13] My father-in-law has had terrible problems getting up and down stairs since his knee\noperation last April.\nW: I'm sorry to hear that. And if you need any help to find transportation for the whole family, we can definitely\nrecommend someone for you.\nM:We were thinking of renting a car, but we will explore all the options available for sure.[14] So, yes, that would be\nvery helpful in comparing prices. We're also wondering what tours and day trips are available.\nW: We have bunches of brochures here. [15] I would recommend getting out on a boat trip. The kids will love it and\nthere are so many islands nearby to explore. There's also a great night market further into town that has all kinds of\nfood and cool little shops selling souvenirs and local jewelry and clothing made by hand.\nM:That all sounds marvelous.\nW: [12] Now, all I need is to photocopy your passports and then I can get you all checked in and show you to your\nrooms.","audioStart":615.8,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"D","expl":"女士提出可推荐交通方式，男士回应“本来考虑租车，但愿意探索所有可选方案，因为这有助于比价”，明确说明原因是“为了比价”，D项正确。A项“取悦岳父母”、B项“给孩子找最好的旅行”、C项“满足好奇心”均未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中“will do”的目的细节捕捉，关注“so”引导的结果逻辑。"},{"no":15,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"15","options":{"A":"Visit a local art gallery.","B":"Go on a boat trip.","C":"Take some photos of the islands.","D":"Try her hand-made clothing."},"script":"W: Hi, there. How are you today? [12] Do you have a reservation with us already?\nM: Good afternoon. [12] Yes, we reserved our rooms yesterday morning on your website for three nights. The name's\nPatterson.\nW: Okay. Let me have a look. Yes, we have it here. You brought the whole family with you, I see\nM: Yes, the two kids, my wife and I, and her parents too.\nW: Great. So we have a family room for you and your wife and the kids, and another double room for your parents-\nin-law. They are right next to each other on the ground floor, since you mentioned in your message that they have\ntrouble with stairs.\nM: That's wonderful. [13] My father-in-law has had terrible problems getting up and down stairs since his knee\noperation last April.\nW: I'm sorry to hear that. And if you need any help to find transportation for the whole family, we can definitely\nrecommend someone for you.\nM:We were thinking of renting a car, but we will explore all the options available for sure.[14] So, yes, that would be\nvery helpful in comparing prices. We're also wondering what tours and day trips are available.\nW: We have bunches of brochures here. [15] I would recommend getting out on a boat trip. The kids will love it and\nthere are so many islands nearby to explore. There's also a great night market further into town that has all kinds of\nfood and cool little shops selling souvenirs and local jewelry and clothing made by hand.\nM:That all sounds marvelous.\nW: [12] Now, all I need is to photocopy your passports and then I can get you all checked in and show you to your\nrooms.","audioStart":615.8,"groupNote":"Questions 12 to 15","answer":"B","expl":"男士咨询旅行项目时，女士明确推荐“乘船出游”，并补充“孩子会喜欢，且附近有很多岛屿可探索”，B项正确。C项“拍岛屿照片”未提及（仅提到探索岛屿）；D项“试穿手工服装”与原文“夜市有手工服装售卖”不符；A项“参观当地美术馆”未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力对话中建议类内容的细节定位，关注“recommend”引导的核心建议。"},{"no":16,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"16","options":{"A":"They are more intelligent than many of us.","B":"They have already become our new friends.","C":"They have begun to affect our social behavior.","D":"They play increasingly more important roles."},"script":"[16] Artificial intelligence agents play ever more influential roles in our lives. They do everything from suggesting\nnew friends to recommending purchases. They're even beginning to drive our cars. Another role that they are expected\nto take over is negotiating on our behalf in commercial transactions or legal disputes. [17] So, it's important to know\nwhether using an artificial intelligence agent might affect how we negotiate. Research indicates that it does. In a new\nstudy, participants were told to imagine that they were negotiating for something important to them, like a house. Next,\nthey were told either that they would negotiate for themselves, or they would program an artificial intelligence agent\nto negotiate for them. Participants then completed a survey indicating how tough, deceptive and pleasant or otherwise\nthey wanted to be, or wanted their agent to be, in the negotiations. For example, participants could choose to be tough\nby making an opening demand far greater than what they'd be willing to accept. They could also choose to express\nsympathy with their opponent to appear pleasant. But they could also indicate that they or their agent—would strategically express anger toward the opponent to gain advantage, or they could opt to convey dissatisfaction with the encounter so that the other party would think they were losing interest. These are both examples of deceptive strategies. [18] Participants were more willing to employ deceptive strategies when assigned an agent to negotiate on their behalf.","audioStart":835.8,"groupNote":"Questions 16 to 18","answer":"D","expl":"短文开头明确提到“人工智能代理在我们的生活中扮演着比以往任何时候都更有影响力的角色”，即“发挥着越来越重要的作用”，D项正确。A项“比我们许多人聪明”未提及；B项“成为我们的新朋友”与原文“推荐新朋友”不符；C项“影响社交行为”未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文主旨细节捕捉，关注开头对事物作用的概括性描述。"},{"no":17,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"17","options":{"A":"Whether it might have any effect on the way we negotiate.","B":"Whether it might actually outperform human negotiators.","C":"Whether it can facilitate business transactions.","D":"Whether it can speed up legal procedures."},"script":"[16] Artificial intelligence agents play ever more influential roles in our lives. They do everything from suggesting\nnew friends to recommending purchases. They're even beginning to drive our cars. Another role that they are expected\nto take over is negotiating on our behalf in commercial transactions or legal disputes. [17] So, it's important to know\nwhether using an artificial intelligence agent might affect how we negotiate. Research indicates that it does. In a new\nstudy, participants were told to imagine that they were negotiating for something important to them, like a house. Next,\nthey were told either that they would negotiate for themselves, or they would program an artificial intelligence agent\nto negotiate for them. Participants then completed a survey indicating how tough, deceptive and pleasant or otherwise\nthey wanted to be, or wanted their agent to be, in the negotiations. For example, participants could choose to be tough\nby making an opening demand far greater than what they'd be willing to accept. They could also choose to express\nsympathy with their opponent to appear pleasant. But they could also indicate that they or their agent—would strategically express anger toward the opponent to gain advantage, or they could opt to convey dissatisfaction with the encounter so that the other party would think they were losing interest. These are both examples of deceptive strategies. [18] Participants were more willing to employ deceptive strategies when assigned an agent to negotiate on their behalf.","audioStart":835.8,"groupNote":"Questions 16 to 18","answer":"A","expl":"短文提到“了解使用人工智能代理是否会影响我们的谈判方式是很重要的”，后续详细介绍相关研究，说明研究目的是“探究它是否可能对我们的谈判方式产生影响”，A项正确。B项“胜过人类谈判者”、C项“便利商业交易”、D项“加快法律程序”均未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文研究目的的细节定位，关注“it's important to know whether”引导的核心问题。"},{"no":18,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"18","options":{"A":"Choose to be tough.","B":"Sympathize with their opponent.","C":"Use deceptive strategies.","D":"Appear to be pleasant."},"script":"[16] Artificial intelligence agents play ever more influential roles in our lives. They do everything from suggesting\nnew friends to recommending purchases. They're even beginning to drive our cars. Another role that they are expected\nto take over is negotiating on our behalf in commercial transactions or legal disputes. [17] So, it's important to know\nwhether using an artificial intelligence agent might affect how we negotiate. Research indicates that it does. In a new\nstudy, participants were told to imagine that they were negotiating for something important to them, like a house. Next,\nthey were told either that they would negotiate for themselves, or they would program an artificial intelligence agent\nto negotiate for them. Participants then completed a survey indicating how tough, deceptive and pleasant or otherwise\nthey wanted to be, or wanted their agent to be, in the negotiations. For example, participants could choose to be tough\nby making an opening demand far greater than what they'd be willing to accept. They could also choose to express\nsympathy with their opponent to appear pleasant. But they could also indicate that they or their agent—would strategically express anger toward the opponent to gain advantage, or they could opt to convey dissatisfaction with the encounter so that the other party would think they were losing interest. These are both examples of deceptive strategies. [18] Participants were more willing to employ deceptive strategies when assigned an agent to negotiate on their behalf.","audioStart":835.8,"groupNote":"Questions 16 to 18","answer":"C","expl":"短文结尾明确指出“当被指派人工智能代理代表谈判时，参与者更愿意采用迷惑性策略”，C项正确。A项“选择强硬”、B项“同情对手”、D项“显得友善”均是谈判中的具体行为，但非“被指派代理时的倾向”，排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文研究结论的细节提取，关注“when assigned... participants were more willing to...”的逻辑关系。"},{"no":19,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"19","options":{"A":"They were perceived differently by some academics.","B":"They still existed six months after the course ended.","C":"They varied greatly among the course participants.","D":"They were only measurable within seven weeks."},"script":"New research has studied the effects of a seven-week healthy cooking course. Academics measured the program's\neffect on cooking confidence and self-perceived mental health. Researchers also measured participants'overall\nsatisfaction around cooking and diet-related behaviors.\nWhat were the findings? Course participants saw significant improvements in general health. They also reported\nimproved mental health and subjective vitality immediately after the program. [19] These benefits remained six months\nafter the completion of the course.\nWhat caused these improvements? Researchers have previously found a link between eating more fruits and vegetables, and improved longer term mental health. This would imply that the participants in the current study may have felt better due to improved diet.\nHowever, the study showed participants'mental health improved even if their reported diet did not change after\ncompleting the program. Also, the mental health benefits were equal among participants who were overweight or obese,\nand those in a healthy weight range. [20] This suggests a link between cooking confidence and satisfaction around\ncooking, and mental health benefits.\n[21] Who benefits most from learning to cook? Gender plays a part. At the start of the program, 77 per cent of\nfemale participants were confident about cooking, but just 23 per cent of males were confident. At the end of the\nprogram, cooking confidence and skills were equal across both counterparts. This change in confidence could lead to a\ngender balance in home cooking.This in turn could reduce consumption of unhealthy,high-calorie processed meals.","audioStart":1015.6,"groupNote":"Questions 19 to 21","answer":"B","expl":"短文提到“参与者在课程结束后立即报告了心理健康和主观活力的改善，这些好处在课程完成六个月后仍然存在”，B项与原文一致。A项“学者看法不同”、C项“参与者间差异大”、D项“仅七周内可测量”均未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文研究结果的细节捕捉，关注时间状语引导的持续性信息。"},{"no":20,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"20","options":{"A":"They can be easily seen among participants in a healthy weight range.","B":"They should be attributed to participants' change in diet behaviors.","C":"They are linked to cooking confidence and cooking satisfaction.","D":"They actually result from eating more fruits and vegetables."},"script":"New research has studied the effects of a seven-week healthy cooking course. Academics measured the program's\neffect on cooking confidence and self-perceived mental health. Researchers also measured participants'overall\nsatisfaction around cooking and diet-related behaviors.\nWhat were the findings? Course participants saw significant improvements in general health. They also reported\nimproved mental health and subjective vitality immediately after the program. [19] These benefits remained six months\nafter the completion of the course.\nWhat caused these improvements? Researchers have previously found a link between eating more fruits and vegetables, and improved longer term mental health. This would imply that the participants in the current study may have felt better due to improved diet.\nHowever, the study showed participants'mental health improved even if their reported diet did not change after\ncompleting the program. Also, the mental health benefits were equal among participants who were overweight or obese,\nand those in a healthy weight range. [20] This suggests a link between cooking confidence and satisfaction around\ncooking, and mental health benefits.\n[21] Who benefits most from learning to cook? Gender plays a part. At the start of the program, 77 per cent of\nfemale participants were confident about cooking, but just 23 per cent of males were confident. At the end of the\nprogram, cooking confidence and skills were equal across both counterparts. This change in confidence could lead to a\ngender balance in home cooking.This in turn could reduce consumption of unhealthy,high-calorie processed meals.","audioStart":1015.6,"groupNote":"Questions 19 to 21","answer":"C","expl":"短文提到“即使参与者饮食未变，心理健康仍有改善；且超重/肥胖参与者与健康体重参与者的心理健康益处相同，这表明烹饪自信、烹饪满意度与心理健康益处之间存在联系”，C项正确。B项“归因于饮食变化”、D项“源于多吃水果蔬菜”与原文“饮食未变仍有改善”相悖；A项“健康体重参与者易见”未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文研究结论的逻辑推导，排除与原文相悖的选项。"},{"no":21,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"21","options":{"A":"Gender.","B":"Confidence.","C":"Health.","D":"Practice."},"script":"New research has studied the effects of a seven-week healthy cooking course. Academics measured the program's\neffect on cooking confidence and self-perceived mental health. Researchers also measured participants'overall\nsatisfaction around cooking and diet-related behaviors.\nWhat were the findings? Course participants saw significant improvements in general health. They also reported\nimproved mental health and subjective vitality immediately after the program. [19] These benefits remained six months\nafter the completion of the course.\nWhat caused these improvements? Researchers have previously found a link between eating more fruits and vegetables, and improved longer term mental health. This would imply that the participants in the current study may have felt better due to improved diet.\nHowever, the study showed participants'mental health improved even if their reported diet did not change after\ncompleting the program. Also, the mental health benefits were equal among participants who were overweight or obese,\nand those in a healthy weight range. [20] This suggests a link between cooking confidence and satisfaction around\ncooking, and mental health benefits.\n[21] Who benefits most from learning to cook? Gender plays a part. At the start of the program, 77 per cent of\nfemale participants were confident about cooking, but just 23 per cent of males were confident. At the end of the\nprogram, cooking confidence and skills were equal across both counterparts. This change in confidence could lead to a\ngender balance in home cooking.This in turn could reduce consumption of unhealthy,high-calorie processed meals.","audioStart":1015.6,"groupNote":"Questions 19 to 21","answer":"A","expl":"短文明确提问“谁从学习烹饪中获益最多？”，随后回答“性别起了一定作用”，并通过男女初始烹饪信心差异及课程后的变化佐证，A项正确。B项“信心”、C项“健康”是文中提及的相关概念但非“决定因素”；D项“实践”未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文核心问题与答案的直接定位，关注设问句及后续回应。"},{"no":22,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"22","options":{"A":"It keeps others away.","B":"It causes discomfort.","C":"It remains visible.","D":"It varies in size."},"script":"[22] What is personal space? We often think of it as an invisible bubble of space surrounding us that others can't\nenter, without causing discomfort. Research shows, however, that we actually have bubbles of different sizes. Each of\nthese bubbles applies to a different set of people.\nThe smallest zone, called \"intimate space\", extends outward from our bodies 18 inches in every direction\nOnly family, pets and one's closest friends may enter it. [23]A mere acquaintance entering our intimate space makes\nus uncomfortable. Next is the bubble called personal space\", extending from 1.5 feet to 4 feet away. Friends and\nacquaintances can comfortably occupy this zone, especially during informal conversations, but strangers are forbidden.\n[24] Extending from 4 to 12 feet away from us is “\"social space\". Here people feel comfortable conducting routine\nsocial interactions with new acquaintances or total strangers.\nThose are the average sizes of American's personal bubbles, anyway. It is important to keep in mind that personal\nspace varies depending on culture and context. Furthermore, there are significant individual differences. As we all\nknow, cultural or individual differences in personal bubble diameters are all too often the cause of discomfort. How did\nthese personal bubbles arise? According to research, we begin to develop our individual sense of personal space around\nage 3 or 4. [25] The sizes of our bubbles are fixed by our teens. These bubbles are constructed and monitored by the\nbrain region involved in fear.","audioStart":1204.4,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"D","expl":"短文开头提到“我们通常认为个人空间是无形泡泡，但研究表明，我们实际上有不同大小的泡泡”，即“个人空间大小各不相同”，D项正确。A项“让他人远离”、B项“引起不适”是个人空间被侵犯的结果而非研究结论；C项“仍然可见”与原文“无形泡泡”相悖，排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文研究结论的细节提取，区分“普遍认知”与“研究发现”。"},{"no":23,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"23","options":{"A":"It makes us feel uncomfortable.","B":"It renders the acquaintance a stranger.","C":"It brings the acquaintance closer to us.","D":"It causes the bubble around us to vanish."},"script":"[22] What is personal space? We often think of it as an invisible bubble of space surrounding us that others can't\nenter, without causing discomfort. Research shows, however, that we actually have bubbles of different sizes. Each of\nthese bubbles applies to a different set of people.\nThe smallest zone, called \"intimate space\", extends outward from our bodies 18 inches in every direction\nOnly family, pets and one's closest friends may enter it. [23]A mere acquaintance entering our intimate space makes\nus uncomfortable. Next is the bubble called personal space\", extending from 1.5 feet to 4 feet away. Friends and\nacquaintances can comfortably occupy this zone, especially during informal conversations, but strangers are forbidden.\n[24] Extending from 4 to 12 feet away from us is “\"social space\". Here people feel comfortable conducting routine\nsocial interactions with new acquaintances or total strangers.\nThose are the average sizes of American's personal bubbles, anyway. It is important to keep in mind that personal\nspace varies depending on culture and context. Furthermore, there are significant individual differences. As we all\nknow, cultural or individual differences in personal bubble diameters are all too often the cause of discomfort. How did\nthese personal bubbles arise? According to research, we begin to develop our individual sense of personal space around\nage 3 or 4. [25] The sizes of our bubbles are fixed by our teens. These bubbles are constructed and monitored by the\nbrain region involved in fear.","audioStart":1204.4,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"A","expl":"短文提到“亲密空间仅家人、宠物和最亲密朋友可进入，仅仅是相识之人进入会让我们感到不舒服”，A项正确。B项“使相识之人变陌生人”、C项“拉近距离”、D项“导致泡泡消失”均未提及，排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文分类信息的细节定位，关注“intimate space”的使用规则。"},{"no":24,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"24","options":{"A":"In personal space.","B":"In social space.","C":"Within a distance of 18 inches.","D":"Over 2 feet away from one another."},"script":"[22] What is personal space? We often think of it as an invisible bubble of space surrounding us that others can't\nenter, without causing discomfort. Research shows, however, that we actually have bubbles of different sizes. Each of\nthese bubbles applies to a different set of people.\nThe smallest zone, called \"intimate space\", extends outward from our bodies 18 inches in every direction\nOnly family, pets and one's closest friends may enter it. [23]A mere acquaintance entering our intimate space makes\nus uncomfortable. Next is the bubble called personal space\", extending from 1.5 feet to 4 feet away. Friends and\nacquaintances can comfortably occupy this zone, especially during informal conversations, but strangers are forbidden.\n[24] Extending from 4 to 12 feet away from us is “\"social space\". Here people feel comfortable conducting routine\nsocial interactions with new acquaintances or total strangers.\nThose are the average sizes of American's personal bubbles, anyway. It is important to keep in mind that personal\nspace varies depending on culture and context. Furthermore, there are significant individual differences. As we all\nknow, cultural or individual differences in personal bubble diameters are all too often the cause of discomfort. How did\nthese personal bubbles arise? According to research, we begin to develop our individual sense of personal space around\nage 3 or 4. [25] The sizes of our bubbles are fixed by our teens. These bubbles are constructed and monitored by the\nbrain region involved in fear.","audioStart":1204.4,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"B","expl":"短文明确说明“4到12英尺的距离是‘社交空间’，在这里，人们与新认识的人或陌生人进行日常社交会感到舒适”，B项正确。A项“个人空间”适用于朋友和熟人；C项“18英寸内”是亲密空间；D项“超过2英尺”范围模糊（社交空间是4-12英尺），排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文空间分类与适用场景的对应，精准匹配不同空间的距离与功能。"},{"no":25,"part":"listening","kind":"mcq","stem":"25","options":{"A":"When we begin to feel fear.","B":"When we develop a sense of space.","C":"When we are 3 or 4 years old.","D":"When we enter our teens."},"script":"[22] What is personal space? We often think of it as an invisible bubble of space surrounding us that others can't\nenter, without causing discomfort. Research shows, however, that we actually have bubbles of different sizes. Each of\nthese bubbles applies to a different set of people.\nThe smallest zone, called \"intimate space\", extends outward from our bodies 18 inches in every direction\nOnly family, pets and one's closest friends may enter it. [23]A mere acquaintance entering our intimate space makes\nus uncomfortable. Next is the bubble called personal space\", extending from 1.5 feet to 4 feet away. Friends and\nacquaintances can comfortably occupy this zone, especially during informal conversations, but strangers are forbidden.\n[24] Extending from 4 to 12 feet away from us is “\"social space\". Here people feel comfortable conducting routine\nsocial interactions with new acquaintances or total strangers.\nThose are the average sizes of American's personal bubbles, anyway. It is important to keep in mind that personal\nspace varies depending on culture and context. Furthermore, there are significant individual differences. As we all\nknow, cultural or individual differences in personal bubble diameters are all too often the cause of discomfort. How did\nthese personal bubbles arise? According to research, we begin to develop our individual sense of personal space around\nage 3 or 4. [25] The sizes of our bubbles are fixed by our teens. These bubbles are constructed and monitored by the\nbrain region involved in fear.","audioStart":1204.4,"groupNote":"Questions 22 to 25","answer":"D","expl":"短文结尾提到“我们的个人空间大小在青少年时期固定下来”，D项正确。A项“开始感到恐惧时”未提及；B项“培养空间意识”、C项“三四岁时”是个人空间意识的发展时间而非大小固定时间，排除。 涉及知识点：听力短文时间节点的细节辨析，区分“发展时间”与“固定时间”。"},{"no":26,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 26 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"focus","answer":"D","expl":"focus 详解： 语法判断：空格所在句缺少谓语，主语为they，时态为一般现在时，故需填动词原形，且能与on构成合理搭配。 语义判断：首句提到人们改善健康的常见方式（健康饮食、改善睡眠、定期锻炼），空格句意为“这些行为都很重要，但它们都侧重于身体健康”。focus on意为“专注于，集中于”，符合句意，故答案为focus。 涉及知识点：动词短语搭配（focus on）、一般现在时的谓语动词用法。"},{"no":27,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 27 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"overall","answer":"F","expl":"overall 详解： 语法判断：空格前为介词to，后为名词well-being，故需填形容词或分词修饰名词。 语义判断：句子强调“社交健康对总体健康同样重要，甚至更为重要”，代入选项后，overall（总体的；全面的）符合句意，故答案为overall。 涉及知识点：形容词作定语的用法、词汇辨析（overall的语义理解）。"},{"no":28,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 28 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"indicator","answer":"E","expl":"indicator 详解： 语法判断：空格前为a better，后为介词of，故需填名词。 语义判断：句子意为“一个人社交圈的强度是其自述的压力、幸福感以及健康水平更好的标志”，根据中文表达习惯，此处需名词动译（“是……更好的标志”转译为“更能反映……”），indicator（标志；迹象）符合句意，故答案为indicator。 涉及知识点：名词用法、翻译中的词性转换技巧。"},{"no":29,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 29 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"quantified","answer":"K","expl":"详解： 语法判断：空格前为定冠词the，后为名词self，故需填形容词或分词修饰名词。 语义判断：句子提到“无穷无尽的健康数据所代表的‘量化的自我’”，quantified（量化的）修饰self构成“quantified self”（量化的自我），符合句意，故答案为quantified。 涉及知识点：过去分词作定语的用法、固定表达（quantified self）。"},{"no":30,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 30 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"story","answer":"N","expl":"story 详解： 语法判断：空格位于句末，前为the whole，故需填名词。 语义判断：句子意为“量化的自我并不能说明全部情况”，the whole story意为“一切；全部情况”，为固定搭配，符合句意，故答案为story。 涉及知识点：固定短语搭配（the whole story）、名词语义辨析。"},{"no":31,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 31 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"reflected","answer":"M","expl":"reflected 详解： 语法判断：空格前为主语和be动词，后为介词短语，故需填形容词作表语或动词过去分词构成被动语态。 语义判断：句子意为“‘我’是谁、有哪些活动、有哪些社交网络等方面都没有在这些评估中反映出来”，reflected（反映；显示）符合句意，故答案为reflected。 涉及知识点：被动语态的用法、动词语义理解（reflected的搭配）。"},{"no":32,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 32 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"prior","answer":"H","expl":"prior 详解： 语法判断：空格前为介词of，后为名词research，故需填形容词或分词修饰名词。 语义判断：句子意为“这一观点得到了大量先前研究的支持”，prior（先前的，较早的）符合句意，故答案为prior。 涉及知识点：形容词作定语的用法、词汇辨析（prior的语义理解）。"},{"no":33,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 33 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"strongly","answer":"O","expl":"strongly 详解： 语法判断：空格所在句结构完整，故需填副词修饰谓语动词。 语义判断：句子意为“社交支持与更好的身心健康密切相关”，be strongly associated with...意为“与……密切相关”，strongly（强烈地；坚决地）符合句意，故答案为strongly。 涉及知识点：副词修饰动词的用法、固定搭配（be associated with）。"},{"no":34,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 34 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"recovery","answer":"L","expl":"recovery 详解： 语法判断：空格前为improve illness，后为rates，故需填名词或分词与illness一起修饰rates。 语义判断：句子列举社交生活的积极影响，“提高疾病康复率”为固定表达，illness recovery rates意为“疾病康复率”，recovery（恢复；痊愈）符合句意，故答案为recovery。 涉及知识点：名词作定语的用法、固定短语（illness recovery rates）。"},{"no":35,"part":"reading","kind":"cloze","stem":"第 35 空","options":{},"passage":"cloze","answerText":"especially","answer":"C","expl":"详解： 语法判断：空格所在句结构完整，故需填副词修饰整个句子。 语义判断：句子意为“这是一个严重的问题，尤其是在许多国家，孤独正成为普遍的公共健康问题”，especially（尤其；特别）用于强调递进关系，符合句意，故答案为especially。 涉及知识点：副词的语义功能（强调递进）、词汇辨析。"},{"no":36,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"36. Research has found the use of digital technology benefits not only teenagers' social lives but also their studies.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"C","expl":"定位解析：由题干关键信息“the use of digital technology、not only...but also、teenagers' social lives、studies”定位到C段。C段前两句提到“研究年轻人的社会科学家发现，他们对数码的使用可以是创造性的，甚至是有益的，不仅从社交生活来说是这样，而且从教育方面来说也是如此”，题干是对该内容的概括（the use of digital technology对应their digital use，not only...but also对应not just in terms of...but...，studies对应education），故答案为C。 涉及知识点：同义替换技巧、段落主旨概括能力。"},{"no":37,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"37. It is urgent that schools teach kids how to verify online information.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"L","expl":"定位解析：由题干关键信息“urgent、schools、teach、verify online information”定位到L段。L段第三句提到“研究表明，学生们不会在网上核实信息的真实性——‘智能搜索’是学校迫切需要教授的一项技能”，题干是该句的同义转述（urgent对应urgently，verify online information对应fact-check information online），故答案为L。 涉及知识点：同义替换技巧（关键词对应）、细节信息定位能力。"},{"no":38,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"38. Students now write longer and more complex essays than their counter","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"H","expl":"定位解析：由题干关键信息“longer and more complex essays、the error rates remain unchanged”定位到H段。G段末尾提到“斯坦福大学学者收集的1917年以来‘新生作文’错误率数据与今天几乎相同”，H段首句接着说“即使错误率保持稳定，学生的作文在长度和复杂性方面都有了很大的发展”，题干与此同义（longer and more complex essays对应essays have blossomed in size and complexity，the error rates remain unchanged对应error rates stayed stable），故答案为H。 涉及知识点：段落衔接信息整合、同义替换（短语层面）。"},{"no":39,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"39. Newspaper reports of teenagers give a false picture of their behaviour.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"A","expl":"定位解析：由题干关键信息“Newspaper reports、teenagers、give a false picture、behaviour”定位到A段。A段提到“报纸上充斥着关于青少年吸毒成瘾、攻击性行为的可怕报道，但这些报道没有真正反映出青少年日常社交行为的多数情况”，题干是对该内容的概括（reports对应accounts，give a false picture对应do not really reflect），故答案为A。 涉及知识点：主旨概括能力、语义转换（否定表达与“歪曲”的对应）。"},{"no":40,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"40. Parents are advised to mind their own digital behaviour and set a good example for their kids.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"M","expl":"定位解析：由题干关键信息“Parents、are advised to、mind their own digital behaviour、set a good example”定位到M段。M段提到“最好的应对方法是适度，树立良好的行为榜样是关键，家长应注意自己的行为”，题干是对该内容的概括（advised对应advice，mind their own digital behaviour对应be careful about our own behaviour），故答案为M。 涉及知识点：段落主旨提炼、同义替换（动词短语层面）。"},{"no":41,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"41. Contrary to parents’ belief, kids try hard to leave as few traces as possible on the web.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"E","expl":"定位解析：由题干关键信息“Contrary to parents' belief、leave as few traces as possible on the web”定位到E段。E段提到“家长们担心孩子不在意隐私是错误的，孩子们会花时间更改设置、使用阅后即焚工具减少分享痕迹”，题干是对该内容的概括（Contrary to parents' belief对应Parents are wrong to...，leave as few traces as possible对应minimise their traces和so that no traces remain），故答案为E。 涉及知识点：细节信息整合、语义转换（否定家长观点与“相反”的对应）。"},{"no":42,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"42. Students' ability to learn formal writing is unlikely to be affected by texting.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"I","expl":"定位解析：由题干关键信息“Students' ability、formal writing、unlikely to be affected、texting”定位到I段。I段最后一句提到“教青少年正式写作仍至关重要，但发短信可能不会破坏他们学习正式写作的能力”，题干是该句的同义转述（Students' ability to learn formal writing对应their ability to learn it，unlikely to be affected by texting对应texting probably isn't destroying），故答案为I。 涉及知识点：同义替换（否定式与“不受影响”的对应）、细节定位能力。"},{"no":43,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"43. Historically, new technologies have always caused great fears among parents.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"B","expl":"定位解析：由题干关键信息“new technologies、always、caused、great fears、parents”定位到B段。B段第一句提到“新技术总是会引起一代人的恐慌，这通常更多地与成年人的恐惧有关”，并举例20世纪30年代家长担心广播、80年代担心索尼随身听，题干是对该内容的概括（caused great fears among parents对应provoke generational panic、adult fears），故答案为B。 涉及知识点：历史案例整合、主旨概括能力。"},{"no":44,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"44. The reading culture was seriously affected by cable television some four decades ago.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"J","expl":"定位解析：由题干关键信息“reading culture、seriously affected、cable television、some four decades ago”定位到J段。J段提到“20世纪80年代是有线电视，而不是互联网，对阅读文化造成了打击”，20世纪80年代对应“大约40年前”，题干是该句的同义转述（seriously affected对应struck a blow at，some four decades ago对应in the 1980s），故答案为J。 涉及知识点：时间转换（年代与“几十年前”的对应）、同义替换（动词短语层面）。"},{"no":45,"part":"reading","kind":"matching","stem":"45. Teachers say that kids' writing is too casual, using language characteristic of text messages.","options":{},"passage":"matching","answer":"G","expl":"定位解析：由题干关键信息“Teachers say、kids writing、too casual、text messages”定位到G段。G段第三句提到“老师们说，孩子们在写作中使用过于随意的语言和短信语言”，题干是该句的同义转述（too casual对应overly casual，using language characteristic of text messages对应use...text-speak in writing），故答案为G。 涉及知识点：细节定位、同义替换（词汇与短语的对应）。"},{"no":46,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"46. What do we learn from the passage about Red Rum the racehorse?","options":{"A":"He captured public affection long before he won national titles.","B":"He won enormous fame and love from British people.","C":"He became a myth three decades after his retirement.","D":"He owed his great success to several well-known horse trainers."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"B","expl":"定位解析：根据题干关键词“Red Rum”和出题顺序与原文一致原则，定位到第一段。 语义判断：第一段指出“在赛马历史上，很少有马匹能像红朗姆那样俘获英国民众的喜爱，退役三十年后仍是最著名和最受欢迎的赛马之一”，选项B（“它在英国民众中享有盛誉，颇受喜爱”）与原文表述一致。选项A（“夺冠前就俘获公众喜爱”）与第四段“蝉联冠军后受喜爱”不符；选项C（“退役三十年成神话”）、D（“归功于几位著名驯马师”）原文未提及，故答案为B。 涉及知识点：细节理解、选项排除技巧。"},{"no":47,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"47. What did the serious bone disease in Red Rum's foot mean to himself and his trainer?","options":{"A":"It was simply a hindrance they had to get over to excel.","B":"It was surely a disadvantage though not considered fatal.","C":"It was actually the end of a racing career that had just begun.","D":"It was really a major obstacle hard to overcome on their own."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"A","expl":"定位解析：根据题干关键词“serious bone disease in Red Rum's foot”定位到第三段。 语义判断：第三段提到“对很多马匹和驯马师来说，这意味着赛马生涯未开始就结束，但对于金加和红朗姆来说，这只是成就非凡必须克服的一个障碍”，选项A（“这只是他们成就非凡必须克服的障碍”）与原文一致（hindrance对应obstacle，get over对应overcome，excel对应greatness）。选项B、D原文未提及；选项C是对“其他马匹和驯马师”的描述，并非红朗姆和其驯马师，故答案为A。 涉及知识点：细节区分（不同对象的情况对比）、同义替换（词汇层面）。"},{"no":48,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"48. What does the author say Red Rum did with his power, speed and jumping ability?","options":{"A":"He won his first Grand National title at 12.","B":"He took two firsts successively in 1935-1936.","C":"He surprised sporting fans worldwide in 1973.","D":"He took three Grand National wins in the 1970s."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"D","expl":"定位解析：根据题干关键词“power, speed and jumping ability”定位到第四段。 语义判断：第四段提到“1973年获首个冠军，1974年蝉联”，第五段提到“1977年第三次夺冠”，三次夺冠均在20世纪70年代，选项D（“它在20世纪70年代三次获得英国国家障碍赛马大赛冠军”）正确。选项A（“12岁获首个冠军”）错误，12岁是1977年第三次夺冠；选项B（“1935-1936年蝉联冠军”）是雷诺兹敦的成就；选项C（“1973年震惊世界”）错误，1977年第三次夺冠才震惊世界，故答案为D。 涉及知识点：时间线梳理、细节区分（不同马匹的成就对比）。"},{"no":49,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"49. What did people generally think of Red Rum when he returned to the racecourse in 1977?","options":{"A":"He had already passed the peak of his racing life.","B":"He would have no rival in Grand National steeplechases.","C":"He had lost his charm with the leagues of Grand National fans.","D":"He could be expected to repeat his glory in the prime of his career."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"A","expl":"定位解析：根据题干关键词“in 1977”定位到第五段第二句。 语义判断：第五段第二句指出“1977年红朗姆重返赛场时，人们普遍认为它已经过了黄金赛龄”，选项A（“它已经过了赛马生涯的巅峰”）与原文一致（passed the peak of his racing life对应past his prime）。选项D与原文意思相反；选项B、C原文未提及，故答案为A。 涉及知识点：同义替换（短语层面）、细节定位能力。"},{"no":50,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"50. What became of Red Rum after his career ended?","options":{"A":"He suffered from severe pain in a heel.","B":"He spent almost all his time traveling.","C":"He lived on various charity benefits.","D":"He remained famous and popular."},"passage":"passage1","answer":"D","expl":"定位解析：根据题干关键词“after his career ended”定位到最后一段最后三句。 语义判断：最后一段提到“红朗姆退役后，公众生活和名声并未减少，到全国各地参加活动，是慈善活动和公共活动的热门嘉宾”，选项D（“它仍然很有名，很受欢迎”）符合原文。选项A（“脚后跟剧烈疼痛”）原文未提及退役后仍疼痛；选项B（“几乎所有时间都在旅行”）错误，旅行是为了参加活动；选项C（“靠慈善活动为生”）错误，它是慈善活动的嘉宾，并非以此为生，故答案为D。 涉及知识点：细节理解、选项语义辨析（避免过度解读）。"},{"no":51,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"51. What does the author say businesspeople often do?","options":{"A":"Strive to make sense of some unknown forces.","B":"Avoid being driven by their own feelings.","C":"Draw on a vast wealth of past experience.","D":"Rely on instincts in decision-making."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"D","expl":"定位解析：根据题干关键词“businesspeople often do”定位到第一段。 语义判断：第一段第一句指出“商界人士经常根据自己的个人感受或直觉做决定”，选项D（“依靠直觉进行决策”）与原文一致（rely on instincts对应based on...instincts）。选项A、B、C与原文表述不符，故答案为D。 涉及知识点：同义替换（动词短语层面）、细节定位能力。"},{"no":52,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"52. What should we take into account in order to make informed decisions?","options":{"A":"The likelihood of errors.","B":"Systematic data and analysis.","C":"The complexity of circumstances.","D":"Personal feelings and business strategies."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"B","expl":"定位解析：根据题干关键词“make informed decisions”定位到第三段。 语义判断：第三段第二、三句提到“长期以来，数据和分析一直都与明智的决策联系在一起，它们减少了出错的可能性，增加了成功的机会”，选项B（“系统的数据和分析”）符合原文。选项A、C、D原文未提及，故答案为B。 涉及知识点：细节理解、核心信息提取能力。"},{"no":53,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"53. What should people do in a decision-making process if instincts disagree with data?","options":{"A":"Resolve the dilemma with previous experience.","B":"Figure out which of the two is more reliable.","C":"Combine the two together.","D":"Prioritize instincts over data."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"C","expl":"定位解析：根据题干关键词“instincts disagree with data”定位到第三段最后一句和第四段。 语义判断：第三段提出“直觉与数据冲突时该怎么办”，第四段给出答案“结合使用数据和直觉以达成最佳决策”，选项C（“将两者结合在一起”）与原文一致（Combine the two together对应using data and instincts in conjunction），故答案为C。 涉及知识点：问题与答案定位匹配、同义替换（短语层面）。"},{"no":54,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"54. What should we do when facing various factors that limit the practicality of data?","options":{"A":"Make a rational and systematic analysis.","B":"Explore the most feasible options.","C":"Resort to our inner wisdom.","D":"Apply definitive criteria."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"C","expl":"定位解析：根据题干关键词“limit the practicality of data”定位到倒数第二段。 语义判断：倒数第二段提到“数据实用性受限时，唯一可行的选择是依靠直觉”，结合第五段“内在智慧指的就是直觉”，选项C（“诉诸我们的内在智慧”）与原文一致（Resort to our inner wisdom对应rely on instincts）。选项A、B、D与原文不符，故答案为C。 涉及知识点：语义转换（直觉与内在智慧的对应）、细节推理能力。"},{"no":55,"part":"reading","kind":"mcq","stem":"55. What does it take for decisions to become the best according to the author?","options":{"A":"Applying instincts and data in conjunction.","B":"Assessing all factors when making a choice.","C":"Recalling what has actually worked in the past.","D":"Refraining from trusting instincts arbitrarily."},"passage":"passage2","answer":"A","expl":"定位解析：根据题干关键词“decisions to become the best”和出题顺序与原文一致原则，定位到最后一段。 语义判断：最后一段第一句指出“将直觉和理性分析相结合，可以做出全面的决策”，“全面的决策”即“最好的决定”，选项A（“结合使用直觉和数据”）与原文一致（Applying instincts and data in conjunction对应Combining instinctive and rational analysis），故答案为A。 涉及知识点：主旨句提取、同义替换（短语层面）。"}],"warnings":[]};